A table joined to itself — for hierarchies, adjacency, and pairwise comparison. Aliases become mandatory.
CREATE TABLE employees (id int PRIMARY KEY, name text, manager_id int REFERENCES employees(id));
SELECT e.name AS employee, m.name AS manager
FROM employees e
LEFT JOIN employees m ON m.id = e.manager_id;
-- pairwise, without duplicate mirrored pairs
SELECT a.name, b.name
FROM customers a
JOIN customers b ON a.country = b.country AND a.id < b.id;
The a.id < b.id idiom is how you get unordered pairs exactly once instead of twice plus the self-pair.

-- all three are semi-joins; the planner produces the same shape
SELECT * FROM customers c WHERE EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM orders o WHERE o.customer_id = c.id);
SELECT * FROM customers c WHERE c.id IN (SELECT customer_id FROM orders);
SELECT DISTINCT c.* FROM customers c JOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.id;
Prefer EXISTS. It expresses the intent, never duplicates rows, and needs no DISTINCT to clean up afterwards. The JOIN … DISTINCT form is the one to avoid: it materialises the duplicates and then sorts them away.

WITH paid AS (
SELECT * FROM orders WHERE status = 'paid'
),
by_customer AS (
SELECT customer_id, sum(total) AS revenue FROM paid GROUP BY customer_id
)
SELECT c.name, b.revenue
FROM by_customer b JOIN customers c ON c.id = b.customer_id
ORDER BY b.revenue DESC;
CTEs make a long query readable by naming its stages, and they can be referenced multiple times. Data-modifying CTEs are a PostgreSQL strength:
WITH archived AS (
DELETE FROM orders WHERE status = 'cancelled' RETURNING *
)
INSERT INTO orders_archive SELECT * FROM archived;
All sub-statements of one query see the same snapshot and execute atomically.

CREATE TABLE employees (
id int PRIMARY KEY,
name text NOT NULL,
manager_id int REFERENCES employees(id)
);
INSERT INTO employees VALUES
(1,'Root CEO', NULL),
(2,'VP Eng', 1),
(3,'VP Sales', 1),
(4,'Eng Manager', 2),
(5,'Engineer A', 4),
(6,'Engineer B', 4),
(7,'Sales Rep', 3);
WITH RECURSIVE org AS (
-- seed: everyone with no manager
SELECT id, name, manager_id, 1 AS depth, ARRAY[id] AS path
FROM employees
WHERE manager_id IS NULL
UNION ALL
-- step: everyone reporting to someone already in `org`
SELECT e.id, e.name, e.manager_id, o.depth + 1, o.path || e.id
FROM employees e
JOIN org o ON e.manager_id = o.id
)
SELECT repeat(' ', depth - 1) || name AS org_chart, depth, path
FROM org
ORDER BY path;
org_chart | depth | path
---------------------+-------+-------------
Root CEO | 1 | {1}
VP Eng | 2 | {1,2}
Eng Manager | 3 | {1,2,4}
Engineer A | 4 | {1,2,4,5}
Engineer B | 4 | {1,2,4,6}
VP Sales | 2 | {1,3}
Sales Rep | 3 | {1,3,7}
The path array does double duty: it produces correct depth-first ordering when used as the ORDER BY key, and it is the natural cycle guard.

Write "customers with no orders" as NOT IN, NOT EXISTS, and LEFT JOIN … IS NULL. Run all three against the fixture. Explain the discrepancy, then delete order 13 and re-run.
Solution
NOT IN returns 0 rows; the other two return Kat and Alan. Order 13 has customer_id IS NULL, so the NOT IN list contains NULL and every comparison yields NULL, which WHERE discards. After DELETE FROM orders WHERE id = 13, all three agree.
SELECT name FROM customers WHERE id NOT IN (SELECT customer_id FROM orders);
SELECT name FROM customers c WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM orders o WHERE o.customer_id = c.id);
SELECT c.name FROM customers c LEFT JOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.id WHERE o.id IS NULL;

Return the two highest-value orders per customer using (a) a window function, (b) LATERAL, (c) DISTINCT ON for the single highest. Compare the plans with EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS).
Solution
-- (a)
SELECT * FROM (
SELECT o.*, row_number() OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY total DESC) rn
FROM orders o
) t WHERE rn <= 2;
-- (b)
SELECT c.id, o.* FROM customers c
LEFT JOIN LATERAL (
SELECT * FROM orders o WHERE o.customer_id = c.id ORDER BY o.total DESC LIMIT 2
) o ON true;
-- (c)
SELECT DISTINCT ON (customer_id) * FROM orders ORDER BY customer_id, total DESC;
